Subject: wasix: support multiple epoll registrations per file

The virtual epoll kept one interest registration per file, so adding the
same file to a second epoll instance displaced the first waiter, and one
readiness transition woke only one set. Linux gives each epoll instance
its own registration, and multi-process servers rely on that: a self-
pipe a process wakes itself through sits in several epoll sets, and a
signal handler's write must wake whichever set is being waited on. Keep
an EpollInterestRegistry of independent registrations per file and fan
readiness out to every interested epoll instance. Every subscription
reinstalls a socket source's selector registration, so one that arrives
on a live descriptor - an interest switch after a request - snapshots
the level-triggered readiness it joined.

Dispatch must not run a handler while the registry lock is held. A
handler holds weak references to the epoll set it belongs to, and when
a guest closes that set just as readiness is on its way, the upgrade
the handler makes is the last strong reference: dropping it at the end
of the call drops the set, its subscriptions and their join guards,
and every one of those guards unsubscribes from this registry. Taking
the registry lock again on the same thread would wedge the runtime's
selector, and with it every socket in the process, so each
registration carries its own lock, the registry lock only copies the
registrations out, and the handlers are called with it released. The
dispatcher owns the buffer they are copied into and empties it after
each dispatch, so a steady stream of events allocates nothing and no
registration is held between events. A registration removed while a
dispatch is in flight may still be called by it once, which is
harmless: its handler's weak references fail to upgrade once the epoll
set is gone, and an event it queues for a subscription already deleted
is dropped as stale when the set is drained.

